#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180765 :tutorial/content "The **kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv^2.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms ($\\rm{kg}$) and meters per second ($\\rm{m/s}$), respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$). \n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\rm{kg}$ is moving along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $3t^2\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Let's calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 0^3 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^3.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2) = 2^3 = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =160\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Kinetic energy is a *scalar* quantity.\n\n- You probably noticed that kinetic energy and work have the same units. This is not a coincidence! We'll learn more about that shortly.\n\n- In practical applications, it's often more important to calculate a *change* in the kinetic energy of a particle between two points in time. \n\nThe **change in kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m,$ **initial velocity** $v_0,$ and **final velocity** $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv_1^2 - \\dfrac12mv_0^2.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Kinetic Energy"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180766 :tutorial/content "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity." :tutorial/title "The Work-Energy Principle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180787 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water, and $C$ is a curve that allows water to flow through it (like a fishing net). \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol C$** is a scalar number that tells us how much water is escaping the region bounded by $C.$ \n\nWe have three possible scenarios: \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n![](images/6f/6ff4d006d3fbf4a434f35a13c8591fc166ff6c74adf2b7969752b7541e486df2.png)\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/38/3812148e855a84e3233316acc8bbf59bd7cd802a874c9e4807c6efd7ca6754cf.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\nThe following vector field has zero flux across $C$ because the amount of water flowing in through $C$ equals the amount of water flowing out through $C.$\n\n![](images/56/56aef4f217e9981755826745a97e8389d86b49518ffd7d3ebc75e5ee2dc28f8b.png)\n\nThe following two vector fields also have a zero flux across $C$ because the water flow is tangential to $C$ at every point along $C.$\n\n![](images/63/637677324152af3d428e405c89025f35dbe5d71e2e8f0a0797ef3aca30bae7e1.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180788 :tutorial/content "If $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ The symbol $\\oint\\limits$ means integration over the closed curve.\n\nIt's helpful to spend some time understanding where this definition comes from, so let's derive it using the closed curve $C$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6dfe8a071798c93f19507808596b7d32af90367b2666643d092a7c50afe5e929.png)\n\nFirst, we pick a point $P$ on $C.$ At this point, we draw the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the outward-pointing normal vector $\\mathbf n$ to $C.$\n\n![](images/49/498e1afbaac8bc8297213eae09cc8bf8742b29f45dda1dfafff868c67ebb9de9.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P.$ Now, $\\mathbf F$ is made up of two components:\n\n- a component parallel to $\\mathbf n,$ which contributes to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ and\n\n- a component perpendicular to $\\mathbf n,$ which does *not* contribute to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\nWe're only interested in the contribution made to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ This is given by the scalar projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac3aae49f372908f2f9aeb53e3fc98bff117846fca05a8c696009e443390811c.png)\n\nTherefore, the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{||\\mathbf{n}||}=\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above represents the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at a single point only. Therefore, to calculate the total net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ we sum (integrate) this expression over the entire curve. This gives\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Constructing an Integral Formula for the Flux"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180789 :tutorial/content "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Orientation-Independence"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180790 :tutorial/content "To calculate the flux of a vector field over a smooth, closed curve, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nIf $C=C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_n$ is piecewise smooth, we have to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over each smooth face. That is,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+ \\cdots +\n\\int\\limits_{C_n} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ on $C_i.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf i + (y-x)^3\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C,$ we sum the fluxes of $\\mathbf F$ over each side:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to compute these integrals, let's construct the flux integral across the side $C_4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe side $C_4$ and its outward-pointing unit normal are shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06fd5a2ecbd709e8e835f2a57ef3ada772526366d51e5e16474d9b8635500e8e.png)\n\nSince this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = -\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-x)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{j})+((y-x)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}))\\cdot (-\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}0-(y-x)^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(x-y)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing a similar procedure for the other sides, we can construct the full expression for the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C.$" :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180791 :tutorial/content "Calculating the flux of a vector field across a rectangle or square requires computing four flux integrals. However, we can often simplify our calculations if the vector field displays symmetry.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Let's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65a8c37c64580f7d1f41f32722f20dfee2b8fd98333920a0feae781ea8d9541e.png)\n\nSince the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ is parallel to $C_2$ and $C_4,$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is easy to verify, since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}0\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand similarly for the integral along $C_4.$ \n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through $C_1$ will cancel with the net outflow through $C_3.$ That is\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n-\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Using  Symmetry to Simplify Flux Calculations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193890 :tutorial/content "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193907 :tutorial/content "We can also compute partial derivatives of functions with three or even more variables. \n\nFor example, suppose we have the function $f(x,y,z).$ We find its partial derivatives as follows:\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x},$ we differentiate with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z},$ we differentiate with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ *and* $y$ as constants.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/title "Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)\n$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\n \n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199461 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: (u,v,w) \\longrightarrow \\left(x(u,v,w), \\, y(u,v,w), \\, z(u,v,w) \\right)\n$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^3,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = R,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7875753545cba8babd08fccaf55236c6b32bfc56f43f496343174acc198f1d9.png)\n\n \n\nTo compute the integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R,$ we use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for double integrals." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199465 :tutorial/content "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\frac{{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}}{‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}}{‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖}\\hspace{0.222em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
